          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"dumas-fils-la-dame-aux-camelias-28a4d9a3e7cd3e10\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Armand’s “Parce que je vous aime, pardieu!” presents his alleged love for Olympe as the reason for his proposition, so its core force is a truth-conditional avowal even though the narrative establishes that it is knowingly false. The love clause itself does not undertake a commitment, and its emphatic delivery supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance occurs within a proposal to become Olympe’s lover, but the relational and transactional undertaking is made by the surrounding offer rather than by the love avowal itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Armand directly addresses Olympe in narrated dialogue; the event is retrospectively recounted by Armand.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The avowal is a calculated pretext in a financially explicit sexual bargain. Olympe immediately identifies Armand’s real motive as revenge against Marguerite, and his retrospective narration confirms that he loves Marguerite while feeling disgust toward Olympe. The deception affects sincerity and truth, not the utterance’s truth-conditional mode. “Pardieu!” adds emphatic insistence but does not make the words a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“--Et pourquoi cette proposition? --Parce que je vous aime, pardieu!”\",\"supports\":\"The love utterance is offered as a proposition answering why Armand has made his offer, strongly supporting truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Olympe replies that Armand is actually in love with Marguerite and wants revenge on her by becoming Olympe’s lover.\",\"supports\":\"The addressee interprets the avowal as deceptive and instrumental rather than as a sincere emotional disclosure or independent undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand later states that he loved Marguerite and that, at the very moment of proposing the bargain, Olympe disgusted him.\",\"supports\":\"The retrospective narration confirms the avowal’s falsity while preserving its asserted presentation of love as true.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Armand explicitly offers Olympe three hundred louis if she will keep him there and later calls the arrangement a “marché.”\",\"supports\":\"The commitment or bargain belongs to the surrounding proposal, not independently to the core words “je vous aime.”\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately distinguishes the avowal’s assertive force from both the surrounding transactional proposal and its deceptive motive. No residual core-force dimension requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification treats truth-conditional force as independent of actual truth or sincerity.\"}"
        }
      ],
